---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import LayerTeardownSimulator from '../components/LayerTeardownSimulator.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Sacred Geometry of Sri Yantra — Concurrency & 9 Avaranas"
  description="A mathematical and historical breakdown of the Sri Yantra: 9 primary interlocking triangles, 43 resultant triangles, the Concurrency Problem, and the 9 sacred circuits."
  canonicalPath="/geometry/"
  isDark={false}
>
  <Header isDark={false} />

  <main class="article-page">
    <article class="article-container">
      <header class="article-header">
        <div class="category-tag">Sacred Mathematics & Vedic Science</div>
        <h1 class="display-title article-title">
          The Sacred Geometry of Sri Yantra: The Mathematical Concurrency of 9 Interlocking Triangles
        </h1>
        <p class="article-subtitle">
          An analytical investigation into the 9 primary triangles, 43 secondary polygons, 24 lotus petals, and the notorious Euclidean triple-intersection problem.
        </p>

        <div class="author-meta">
          <span>By <strong>Atharva Mahamuni</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/athmatwt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">@athmatwt</a>)</span>
          <span class="meta-dot">•</span>
          <span>12 Min Read</span>
          <span class="meta-dot">•</span>
          <span>Updated Vedic Research</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="article-content">
        <p class="lead-paragraph">
          The <strong>Sri Yantra</strong> (also known as <em>Sri Chakra</em>) is recognized by mathematicians, physicists, and sacred geometers worldwide as one of the most structurally sophisticated two-dimensional figures in human history. Composed of <strong>9 primary interlocking isosceles triangles</strong> that radiate outward from a dimensionless center, its construction requires resolving multiple simultaneous triple-line intersections—a challenge that baffled geometers for centuries.
        </p>

        <figure class="article-figure">
          <div class="diagram-preview">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 400 400" width="320" height="320" class="vector-yantra" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="190" stroke="#c89547" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="160" stroke="#c89547" stroke-width="1"/>
              <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="125" stroke="#c89547" stroke-width="1"/>
              <!-- 4 Upward Shiva Triangles -->
              <polygon points="200,45 340,290 60,290" stroke="#e88c38" stroke-width="1.4"/>
              <polygon points="200,80 320,250 80,250" stroke="#e88c38" stroke-width="1.3"/>
              <polygon points="200,120 310,320 90,320" stroke="#e88c38" stroke-width="1.3"/>
              <polygon points="200,155 280,225 120,225" stroke="#e88c38" stroke-width="1.2"/>
              <!-- 5 Downward Shakti Triangles -->
              <polygon points="200,355 340,110 60,110" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.4"/>
              <polygon points="200,320 320,150 80,150" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.3"/>
              <polygon points="200,280 310,80 90,80" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.3"/>
              <polygon points="200,245 280,175 120,175" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.2"/>
              <polygon points="200,215 230,190 170,190" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <!-- Central Bindu -->
              <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="3" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#f54e00" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption>Geometric schematic showing the 4 upward Shiva (amber) and 5 downward Shakti (crimson) primary triangles.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>1. The 9 Primary Triangles (Mula Trikonas)</h2>
        <p>
          At the core of the Sri Yantra lie 9 fundamental triangles:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>4 Upward Triangles (Shiva)</strong>: Representing ascending spiritual consciousness, the fire element (<em>Agni</em>), and the divine masculine.</li>
          <li><strong>5 Downward Triangles (Shakti)</strong>: Representing descending creative energy, kinetic power, the water element, and the divine feminine.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because there are 4 upward and 5 downward triangles, the Sri Yantra is deliberately <strong>asymmetrical in static count yet harmonically balanced in dynamic equilibrium</strong>. The universe is not a static crystal; it is in constant, dynamic unfolding.
        </p>

        <h2 id="concurrency">2. The "Concurrency Problem" Explained</h2>
        <p>
          In elementary plane geometry, if you draw three random straight lines across a paper, they will form a triangle between them. For three straight lines to intersect at the <em>exact same point</em> (known in geometry as a <strong>triple intersection or point of concurrency</strong>), their mathematical equations must satisfy an exact algebraic constraint.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the Sri Yantra, there are multiple triple intersections where:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>The vertices of inner triangles must land precisely on the edges of outer triangles.</li>
          <li>Lines belonging to three separate triangles must cross at the exact same coordinate without creating secondary sliver gaps or bent lines.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If drawn naively, small errors compound, leading to mismatched vertices, irregular polygonal slivers, or distorted lines. In modern mathematics, Russian mathematician <strong>Alexey Pavlov</strong> and Dutch researcher <strong>van den Dungen</strong> utilized non-linear simultaneous equations to establish the precise coordinates that produce flawless mathematical concurrency.
        </p>

        <h2>3. The 43 Resultant Triangles</h2>
        <p>
          As the 9 primary triangles interlock, their straight line segments divide the internal circle into <strong>43 smaller secondary triangles</strong> arranged across 5 concentric circuits:
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Level</th>
                <th>Sanskrit Name</th>
                <th>Triangles</th>
                <th>Symbolic Meaning</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Level 1</td>
                <td>Sarva Siddhiprada</td>
                <td>1 (Central)</td>
                <td>The primary downward triad: Will (Iccha), Knowledge (Jnana), Action (Kriya).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Level 2</td>
                <td>Sarvarogahara</td>
                <td>8 Triangles</td>
                <td>Eradication of physical and psychological afflictions; mental balance.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Level 3</td>
                <td>Sarvarakshakara</td>
                <td>10 Triangles</td>
                <td>The 10 vital internal fires of transformation and digestion.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Level 4</td>
                <td>Sarvarthasadhaka</td>
                <td>10 Triangles</td>
                <td>The 10 Pranas (vital life energies flowing through the subtle body).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Level 5</td>
                <td>Sarva Saubhagyadayaka</td>
                <td>14 Triangles</td>
                <td>The 14 primary subtle Nadis (energy meridians).</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>4. The 9 Avaranas (Concentric Circuits)</h2>
        <p>
          The complete Sri Yantra is partitioned into 9 circuits (*Nava Avaranas*). In traditional meditation, the practitioner journeys from the outside physical layer inward to the center:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Trailokya Mohana Chakra (Bhupura)</strong>: The 3-lined square citadel with 4 cardinal T-gates. Represents the earth element and sensory boundary.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarvasa Paripuraka Chakra</strong>: The outer ring of <strong>16 lotus petals</strong>, representing mastery over the 16 sensory desires.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarva Samkshobhana Chakra</strong>: The inner ring of <strong>8 lotus petals</strong>, representing the 8 aspects of primordial Nature (Earth, Water, Fire, Air, Space, Mind, Intellect, Ego).</li>
          <li><strong>Sarva Saubhagyadayaka Chakra</strong>: Ring of 14 triangles.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarvarthasadhaka Chakra</strong>: Outer ring of 10 triangles.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarvarakshakara Chakra</strong>: Inner ring of 10 triangles.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarvarogahara Chakra</strong>: Ring of 8 triangles.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarva Siddhiprada Chakra</strong>: The innermost downward triangle.</li>
          <li><strong>Sarvanandamaya Chakra (Bindu)</strong>: The central dimensionless point of pure, unconditioned bliss.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Interactive 3D Layer Teardown Section (Full Width) -->
    <LayerTeardownSimulator />

    <article class="article-container">
      <div class="article-content">
        <div class="cta-box">
          <h3>Experience the Geometry in Real-Time</h3>
          <p>
            Watch the 9 circuits rotate and breathe in 60FPS with our interactive visualizer and 136.1 Hz Om drone.
          </p>
          <a href={url("/")} class="cta-btn">Open Live Meditation Visualizer</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer isDark={false} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page {
    padding: 3.5rem 1.5rem 5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-canvas);
  }

  .article-container {
    max-width: 780px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .article-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .category-tag {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .article-title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.75rem);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .article-subtitle {
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    color: var(--color-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .author-meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    padding-bottom: 1.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
  }

  .meta-dot {
    color: var(--color-hairline-strong);
  }

  .author-meta a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .article-content {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .lead-paragraph {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .article-figure {
    margin: 3rem 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .diagram-preview {
    background-color: #12110f;
    border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 2rem;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    box-shadow: 0 10px 30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);
  }

  figcaption {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .article-content h2 {
    font-size: 1.625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 2.75rem 0 1rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .article-content p {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .article-content ul, .article-content ol {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .article-content li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .table-wrapper {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  .data-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .data-table th, .data-table td {
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .data-table th {
    background-color: var(--color-surface-card);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .data-table td {
    background-color: #ffffff;
    color: var(--color-body);
  }

  .cta-box {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
    background-color: var(--color-surface-card);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-primary);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 2.25rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .cta-box h3 {
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .cta-box p {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    max-width: 500px;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .cta-btn {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.75rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-color: var(--color-primary);
    color: #ffffff;
    font-weight: 500;
    transition: background-color 0.2s var(--ease-calm);
  }

  .cta-btn:hover {
    background-color: var(--color-primary-active);
  }
</style>
